      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure I agree with this part. A fernet repository is just a set of keys somewhere, and currently that somewhere is on disk. We don\u0027t expect the keys to know what they are suppose to be used for. Instead we leave it to whatever needs the encrypting done. If something needs to have a value encrypted or decrypted, it needs to specifically say which repository it needs to interact with [0] [1]. This makes the fernet token provider responsible for interacting with the repository dedicated to token encryption. The same applies with the fernet credentials provider. The only relationship the current key repository directories have with the system that uses them is the default naming convention (`/etc/keystone/fernet-keys/`, `/etc/keystone/credential-keys/`). But there is nothing stopping someone from swapping those values through config to be something more obscure.\n\nI think having the drivers and keys keep track of what they are encrypting is unnecessary coupling if we already expect manager to interact with the proper key repository.\n\n\n[0] https://github.com/openstack/keystone/blob/686f9d583eaa5f015d6b8b995c2f4243392ffbce/keystone/credential/providers/fernet/core.py#L45-L46\n\n[1] https://github.com/openstack/keystone/blob/686f9d583eaa5f015d6b8b995c2f4243392ffbce/keystone/token/providers/fernet/token_formatters.py#L59-L62",
