        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-12-29T15:48:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What is going to use this api, other managers? The `keystone-manage` CLI? I can see a use case for other managers to be able to pass something to a separate API to encrypt or decrypt it [0], but I\u0027m not sure if exposing key rotation and repository management in an API is the right approach. I could be missing a use case though. The current approach to our file-based repository management is to call utility functions from `keystone-manage` to make new keys, rename them in rotations, and remove them when necessary. I think allowing other storage mechanisms for fernet keys would consist of making the existing utility methods pluggable through config without exposing their management functions through a manager class, but through `keystone-manage`.\n\nThat way, regardless of the fernet key store backend you have defined in your configuration, you should be able to do `keystone-manage fernet_setup` and get a set of file-based keys (like you do today), or a repository of fernet keys somewhere else.\n\n\n[0] https://github.com/openstack/keystone/blob/686f9d583eaa5f015d6b8b995c2f4243392ffbce/keystone/credential/core.py#L68-L70",
